### What is a one-to-one relationship?

A one-to-one relationship means a row on each side maps to at most one row on the other. You model it by putting a foreign key on one table that references the other and making that foreign key unique. Often the child shares the same primary key value as the parent.

```sql
-- At the National Bank of India, each account has exactly one KYC record
CREATE TABLE accounts (
    account_id VARCHAR(20) PRIMARY KEY,
    holder_name VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL,
    balance NUMERIC(15,2) NOT NULL DEFAULT 0.00
);

CREATE TABLE kyc_details (
    account_id VARCHAR(20) PRIMARY KEY,  -- Shared PK = one-to-one
    aadhaar_number VARCHAR(12) NOT NULL,
    pan_number VARCHAR(10) NOT NULL,
    verified_at TIMESTAMP NOT NULL,
    FOREIGN KEY (account_id) REFERENCES accounts(account_id)
);
```

```java
package com.bank.model;

import javax.persistence.*;

@Entity
@Table(name = "accounts")
public class Account {

    @Id
    private String accountId;
    private String holderName;
    private java.math.BigDecimal balance;

    @OneToOne(mappedBy = "account", cascade = CascadeType.ALL)
    private KycDetails kycDetails;
}

@Entity
@Table(name = "kyc_details")
public class KycDetails {

    @Id
    private String accountId;
    private String aadhaarNumber;
    private String panNumber;

    @OneToOne
    @MapsId  // Shares the same PK as Account
    @JoinColumn(name = "account_id")
    private Account account;
}
```

One-to-one is used to split a table for good reasons: separating rarely-used or large columns, isolating sensitive data like KYC details under tighter access control, or extending a table without altering it.

### What is a one-to-many relationship?

A one-to-many relationship means one parent row relates to many child rows. You model it by putting a foreign key on the child table referencing the parent's primary key, with no uniqueness constraint so many children can share the same parent.

```sql
-- One account has many transactions
CREATE TABLE transactions (
    txn_id BIGSERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
    account_id VARCHAR(20) NOT NULL,
    amount NUMERIC(15,2) NOT NULL,
    txn_type VARCHAR(10) NOT NULL,  -- DEBIT or CREDIT
    created_at TIMESTAMP NOT NULL DEFAULT NOW(),
    FOREIGN KEY (account_id) REFERENCES accounts(account_id)
);

CREATE INDEX idx_txn_account ON transactions(account_id);

-- One account → many transactions
SELECT a.holder_name, t.amount, t.txn_type
FROM accounts a
JOIN transactions t ON a.account_id = t.account_id
WHERE a.account_id = 'SAV2001';
```

```java
package com.bank.model;

import javax.persistence.*;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.time.LocalDateTime;
import java.util.List;

@Entity
@Table(name = "accounts")
public class AccountWithTransactions {

    @Id
    private String accountId;
    private String holderName;
    private BigDecimal balance;

    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "account", fetch = FetchType.LAZY)
    private List<Transaction> transactions;
}

@Entity
@Table(name = "transactions")
public class Transaction {

    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
    private Long txnId;
    private BigDecimal amount;
    private String txnType;
    private LocalDateTime createdAt;

    @ManyToOne(fetch = FetchType.LAZY)
    @JoinColumn(name = "account_id")
    private AccountWithTransactions account;  // Many transactions → one account
}
```

This is the most common relationship in any schema: one account to many transactions, one customer to many accounts, one order to many line items.

### How is many-to-one different from one-to-many?

Many-to-one is not a different mechanism. It is the same relationship seen from the child's perspective. From the account you look one-to-many at its transactions; from a transaction you look many-to-one at its account. The foreign key lives on the "many" side in both descriptions.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.sql.Connection;
import java.sql.PreparedStatement;
import java.sql.ResultSet;
import java.sql.SQLException;
import javax.sql.DataSource;

public class RelationshipViewService {

    private final DataSource dataSource;

    public RelationshipViewService(DataSource dataSource) {
        this.dataSource = dataSource;
    }

    // One-to-many view: "Show me all transactions for this account"
    public void showAccountTransactions(String accountId) throws SQLException {
        String sql = "SELECT t.txn_id, t.amount, t.txn_type "
                   + "FROM transactions t WHERE t.account_id = ?";
        try (Connection conn = dataSource.getConnection();
             PreparedStatement ps = conn.prepareStatement(sql)) {
            ps.setString(1, accountId);
            try (ResultSet rs = ps.executeQuery()) {
                while (rs.next()) {
                    System.out.println("Txn " + rs.getLong("txn_id")
                            + ": " + rs.getBigDecimal("amount"));
                }
            }
        }
    }

    // Many-to-one view: "Show me which account this transaction belongs to"
    public String getAccountForTransaction(long txnId) throws SQLException {
        String sql = "SELECT t.account_id FROM transactions t WHERE t.txn_id = ?";
        try (Connection conn = dataSource.getConnection();
             PreparedStatement ps = conn.prepareStatement(sql)) {
            ps.setLong(1, txnId);
            try (ResultSet rs = ps.executeQuery()) {
                return rs.next() ? rs.getString("account_id") : null;
            }
        }
    }
}
```

### What is a many-to-many relationship?

A many-to-many relationship means rows on both sides can relate to many rows on the other. You cannot model this with a single foreign key because neither side can hold a list of references. Instead you introduce a junction table with one foreign key to each side.

```sql
-- Customers can hold many products, products are held by many customers
CREATE TABLE customers (
    customer_id VARCHAR(20) PRIMARY KEY,
    name VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL
);

CREATE TABLE products (
    product_id VARCHAR(20) PRIMARY KEY,
    product_name VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL,
    product_type VARCHAR(50) NOT NULL  -- SAVINGS, CREDIT_CARD, LOAN
);

-- Junction table: the many-to-many link
CREATE TABLE customer_products (
    customer_id VARCHAR(20) NOT NULL,
    product_id VARCHAR(20) NOT NULL,
    acquired_date DATE NOT NULL,
    credit_limit NUMERIC(15,2),  -- Relationship attribute
    PRIMARY KEY (customer_id, product_id),  -- Composite PK
    FOREIGN KEY (customer_id) REFERENCES customers(customer_id),
    FOREIGN KEY (product_id) REFERENCES products(product_id)
);

CREATE INDEX idx_cp_product ON customer_products(product_id);

-- Find all products for Rajesh
SELECT p.product_name, cp.acquired_date, cp.credit_limit
FROM customer_products cp
JOIN products p ON cp.product_id = p.product_id
WHERE cp.customer_id = 'C001';

-- Find all customers who hold a savings account
SELECT c.name, cp.acquired_date
FROM customer_products cp
JOIN customers c ON cp.customer_id = c.customer_id
WHERE cp.product_id = 'SAVINGS_001';
```

```java
package com.bank.model;

import javax.persistence.*;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.time.LocalDate;

@Entity
@Table(name = "customer_products")
public class CustomerProduct {

    @EmbeddedId
    private CustomerProductId id;

    @ManyToOne
    @MapsId("customerId")
    @JoinColumn(name = "customer_id")
    private Customer customer;

    @ManyToOne
    @MapsId("productId")
    @JoinColumn(name = "product_id")
    private Product product;

    private LocalDate acquiredDate;
    private BigDecimal creditLimit;  // Relationship attribute
}

@Embeddable
public class CustomerProductId implements java.io.Serializable {
    private String customerId;
    private String productId;
}
```

The junction table is the natural place to store attributes of the relationship itself, such as when Priya acquired the product or her credit limit.

### What are the rules for indexing relationships?

Foreign keys on the many side and in junction tables should be indexed for join performance. Without an index, a join does a sequential scan on the child table.

```sql
-- Transactions: FK on the many side must be indexed
CREATE INDEX idx_txn_account ON transactions(account_id);

-- Junction table: both FKs should be indexed
-- The composite PK already indexes (customer_id, product_id)
-- But queries by product_id alone need a separate index
CREATE INDEX idx_cp_product ON customer_products(product_id);
```

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.sql.Connection;
import java.sql.PreparedStatement;
import java.sql.ResultSet;
import java.sql.SQLException;
import javax.sql.DataSource;

public class JoinPerformanceService {

    private final DataSource dataSource;

    public JoinPerformanceService(DataSource dataSource) {
        this.dataSource = dataSource;
    }

    // This join is fast because account_id is indexed on transactions
    public void getAccountStatement(String accountId) throws SQLException {
        String sql = "SELECT t.txn_id, t.amount, t.created_at "
                   + "FROM accounts a "
                   + "JOIN transactions t ON a.account_id = t.account_id "
                   + "WHERE a.account_id = ? "
                   + "ORDER BY t.created_at DESC";
        try (Connection conn = dataSource.getConnection();
             PreparedStatement ps = conn.prepareStatement(sql)) {
            ps.setString(1, accountId);
            try (ResultSet rs = ps.executeQuery()) {
                while (rs.next()) {
                    System.out.println(rs.getLong("txn_id") + ": "
                            + rs.getBigDecimal("amount"));
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
```

### What about cascade and delete behavior?

You must decide the cascade and delete behavior deliberately. Should deleting a customer delete their holdings? Should deleting an account delete its KYC record?

```sql
-- CASCADE: deleting an account also deletes its KYC
CREATE TABLE kyc_details_with_cascade (
    account_id VARCHAR(20) PRIMARY KEY,
    aadhaar_number VARCHAR(12) NOT NULL,
    FOREIGN KEY (account_id) REFERENCES accounts(account_id) ON DELETE CASCADE
);

-- RESTRICT: prevent deleting a customer if they have products
CREATE TABLE customer_products_restrict (
    customer_id VARCHAR(20) NOT NULL,
    product_id VARCHAR(20) NOT NULL,
    FOREIGN KEY (customer_id) REFERENCES customers(customer_id) ON DELETE RESTRICT
);

-- SET NULL: if a manager leaves, set managed_by to NULL
CREATE TABLE account_assignments (
    account_id VARCHAR(20) PRIMARY KEY,
    managed_by VARCHAR(20),
    FOREIGN KEY (managed_by) REFERENCES employees(employee_id) ON DELETE SET NULL
);
```

```java
package com.bank.model;

import javax.persistence.*;

@Entity
public class AccountWithCascade {

    @Id
    private String accountId;

    // CASCADE: JPA deletes KYC when account is deleted
    @OneToOne(mappedBy = "account", cascade = CascadeType.ALL, orphanRemoval = true)
    private KycDetails kycDetails;

    // LAZY: transactions are NOT loaded until accessed
    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "account", fetch = FetchType.LAZY)
    private java.util.List<Transaction> transactions;
}
```

### What are common mistakes with relationships?

The most common mistake is missing the foreign key constraint entirely, which allows orphan rows. Another mistake is using a join table for a one-to-many relationship, adding unnecessary complexity.

```sql
-- WRONG: no foreign key constraint, allows orphan transactions
CREATE TABLE transactions (
    txn_id BIGSERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
    account_id VARCHAR(20) -- no FK constraint!
);

-- CORRECT: foreign key enforces referential integrity
CREATE TABLE transactions (
    txn_id BIGSERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
    account_id VARCHAR(20) NOT NULL
        REFERENCES accounts(account_id)
);
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer should prefer explicit junction entities over `@ManyToMany` when the junction table carries its own attributes. They should also understand the N+1 query problem with lazy-loaded relationships and solve it with `JOIN FETCH` or entity graphs.

```java
package com.bank.repository;

import org.springframework.data.jpa.repository.JpaRepository;
import org.springframework.data.jpa.repository.Query;
import com.bank.model.AccountWithTransactions;

public interface AccountRepository extends JpaRepository<AccountWithTransactions, String> {

    // N+1 problem: loading 100 accounts and then lazy-loading
    // transactions for each one generates 101 queries

    // Fix: JOIN FETCH loads account + transactions in one query
    @Query("SELECT a FROM AccountWithTransactions a "
         + "JOIN FETCH a.transactions WHERE a.accountId = :id")
    AccountWithTransactions findWithTransactions(String id);
}
```

```text
Relationship summary:

  One-to-One:    Account ←→ KYC (shared PK or unique FK)
  One-to-Many:   Account → Transactions (FK on child)
  Many-to-One:   Transaction → Account (same FK, child view)
  Many-to-Many:  Customer ←→ Product (junction table)
```
